  "subtitle": "small puff pastry tarts with a lemon-almond filling",
  "excerpt": "Richmond Maids of Honour are small, dome-topped pastry tarts made with puff pastry cases and a filling of ground almonds, curd cheese, and lemon, which puffs slightly during baking and turns golden on top. They have been made in the town of Richmond in Surrey since at least the 18th century and are still sold at Newens, the original bakery in Kew, which has kept the recipe confidential for over a century. This version uses the publicly documented almond-curd filling, which produces a very good result.",
