  "sourceNotes": "Richmond Maids of Honour are associated with Richmond Palace and are said to have been made in Tudor times, though the connection is likely apocryphal. The first documented recipe appears in the 18th century, and Newens bakery in Kew has sold them since at least the 1850s. The original recipe is a trade secret; the documented reconstruction from Marian McNeill and other researchers uses a combination of curd cheese, ground almonds, and lemon, which is the most widely attested version outside the original bakery. The name 'Maids of Honour' refers to a category of small tarts and is not unique to Richmond.",
